  "worked_solution": "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is defined for $n\\ge 1.$ To determine whether ${a}_{n}$ is increasing or decreasing, we can use the following test:\n\n- If a sequence ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* then for all $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}\\ge {a}_{n}\\,\u27f9\\,{a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n- If a sequence ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing,* then for all $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}\\le {a}_{n}\\,\u27f9\\,{a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}\\le 0.\n$$\n\n- If neither of these conditions holds, the sequence is *neither increasing nor decreasing*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the difference ${a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n} & =(n+1)^{2}-5-({n}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =(n+1)^{2}-5-{n}^{2}+5 \\\\  & ={n}^{2}+2n+1-{n}^{2} \\\\  & =2n+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *positive* for $n\\ge 1.$\n\nTo see this concretely, set ${b}_{n}=2n+1.$ Computing the first few terms of ${b}_{n}$ we get\n\n$$\n3,\\,5,\\,7,\\,9,\\,\u2026\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement III is false. Since ${a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
